In Hibernate/JPA, both load()/getOne() (older style) and findById() are used to fetch an entity by its primary key, but they behave differently:

🔹 loadById (or Session.load())

Returns a proxy object (not the actual entity immediately).

The entity is fetched lazily → data is retrieved from DB only when you access a property.

If the entity does not exist in DB → it throws ObjectNotFoundException at access time.

Good when you are sure the entity exists and want to avoid immediate DB hit.

🔹 findById (or EntityManager.find() / Spring Data findById())

Immediately hits the database and returns the entity.

If the entity is not found → it returns null (or Optional.empty() in Spring Data).

No proxy object is returned.

Safer when you are unsure if the record exists.

✅ Summary:

loadById → Lazy (proxy), exception if not found.

findById → Immediate fetch, returns null/Optional if not found.